-- A loop of tail calls through a handler, the twin of the kernel program `loop-tail`
-- (Backend/Kernel.lean, `Shapes.loopTail`): `go` calls itself in tail position, and each turn
-- performs `yld`, whose clause resumes in tail position, so `yld` is a direct call of the clause,
-- no continuation is built, and the loop runs in constant stack. It is for the constant-stack
-- check of `scripts/compile-native.sh` (native, in a stack of 64 KiB).
--
-- 9000 turns, where the kernel's loop has 100000: a recursion is driven by `bounded`, whose
-- budget (`recBudget`, 10000) counts every call of the group, tail calls too, so 10^5 turns are
-- not expressible at the surface yet. A frame of at least 16 bytes per turn would still take
-- over 144 KiB, past the 64 KiB stack.
--
-- Answer: inl (); `live 0`. The kernel's answer is (): `exhausted_to_option` puts it in `inl`,
-- and a spent budget would answer `inr ()`. A lowering that built a continuation per turn without
-- freeing it would leave objects live; one that grew the stack per turn would overflow.

include rec

effect Y { must_resume ctl yld() }

handler pass[A]: Y, A -> A = { return(a) = a; yld(k) = k(()) }

fn go(n: i32) -> 1 = if n == 0 { () } else { yld(); go(n - 1) }

main -> 1 + 1 = with exhausted_to_option[1] { with pass[1] { go(9000) } }
